  caused by nothing but the order of a floating-point summation. Strongest
  available result, and it needs no bare metal.

====================================================================
PROBE 4 — FEASIBILITY OF THE BARE-METAL ARMS ON THE ACTUAL FLEET
====================================================================
B1. NO CROSS-SILICON MECHANISM EXISTS. Same EFI + same dispatched kernel + same
    MXCSR => bit-identical on any IEEE-754-compliant x86_64. Verified by
    exhaustion: PROBE 1 objdump (zero implementation-defined FP instructions) plus
    PROBE 1 FTZ/DAZ null. The "silicon alone diverges" arm is predicted NULL.

B2. THE "SAME KERNEL, TWO SILICONS, BARE METAL" CELL IS NOT BUILDABLE.
    Bare-metal AVX2 machines available: exactly one (Dell i5-5200U).
    N4020 is Goldmont Plus, no AVX — MEASURED, not assumed:
      "GAUNTLET CPU: Intel(R) Celeron(R) N4020 ... simd=SSE2 (scalar fallback)"
      (docs/hardware_logs/gauntlet_hp_stream_n4020_2026-07-14_141531.txt)
    The i5-10210U is the only dev box and does not boot the unikernel.
    Buildable silicon-isolation test: Dell force-scalar vs N4020 native-scalar —
    realizable, and by B1 it returns a null.

B3. NO ARC-EASY SCORER EXISTS ON THE UNIKERNEL.
    grep -in "mc|perplexity|nll|arc_easy|jsonl" aegis-uefi/src/main.rs -> NO MATCHES.
    aegis-eval is hosted-only (std:: in src/main.rs and src/mc.rs).

B4. RUNTIME ON THE N4020 ARM IS DAYS, NOT HOURS.
    MEASURED ARC-Easy n=570 Falcon-E-1B dev box = 23,373.8 s (6.5 h), 92,923
    tokens, 3.98 tok/s (m3_mc_full570_falcon_e_1b_2026-07-18.log).
    MEASURED scalar penalty same silicon (Dell, M7 prefill): 571,637,622 vs
    91,251,533 ticks = 6.27x (m7_baremetal_prompts_postfix_2026-07-29.log).
    MEASURED N4020 vs Dell, identical BitNet gauntlet prefill: 190.5 s firmware /
    55.5 s post-turbo vs Dell 28.3 s. Composed => multiple days of uninterrupted
    UEFI boot on a fanless Celeron, no checkpoint/resume in any code path.

B5. THE ONLY MODEL FAST ENOUGH FOR IRON CANNOT CARRY THE HEADLINE.
    M7 runs 13.8M ticks/token, 214 tok/s on the post-fix Dell (same log as B4), but
    vocab 8192 / window 512 (m7_final_gate_work/export/config.json) is not the
    Falcon-E tokenizer the n=570 items file uses, so the transformers parity gate
    (m3_mc_parity_arc_easy_2026-07-18.log) does not transfer; and a 14.17M-param
    TinyStories model sits at chance on ARC-Easy where binomial SE at n=570,
    p=0.25 is 1.81 pts, so a 1-point delta is 0.55 sigma.

B6. UEFI PROMPT BATCHING DOES NOT EXIST. Interactive read_key loop
    (aegis-uefi/src/main.rs:534), 512-byte line buffer. /gauntlet and /autotest use
    hardcoded prompts and the gauntlet callback discards text (main.rs:601-603).
    Free-prompt path does log full text ("RESPONSE: {}", main.rs:976). A 100-prompt
    cross-machine sweep needs a new no_std PROMPTS.TXT driver + reflash of 2 sticks.

====================================================================
VERDICT / CHEAPEST DECISIVE ORDERING
====================================================================
Invert the candidate. Publish "reduction order, not silicon, is the uncontrolled
variable" — 100% hosted. Use the unikernel for the cheap complementary POSITIVE
claim: a bit-exactness certificate (identical token-stream hash for the same EFI on
Broadwell-U and Goldmont Plus with reduction order pinned to scalar). Minutes of M7
runtime, and it doubles as a memory-map-dependent-UB detector, the highest-value
thing that arm can actually find.

Cheapest decisive ordering (all three done this session, ~1 h):
  1. margin distribution from m3_1b_results_n570.jsonl        (30 s, no inference)
  2. reduction-order perturbation on teacher-forced NLL + A-vs-A bitwise control
                                                              (~20 min, 8 strings)
  3. compare 2 vs 1 -> decide whether the 13 h ARC-Easy sweep is worth it
Result: perturbation 0.05-0.51 nats vs 5th-percentile margin 0.021 nats
        => the sweep IS worth it, expected 2-4 pts, and no iron is required.

CAVEAT THE CANDIDATE MUST CARRY: int8_act is a DEFAULT feature
(aegis-core/Cargo.toml:24). The 1.5e-2 relative NLL perturbation is far above the
1e-4 the kernels agree to, so the headline effect size is probably a property of
the ACTIVATION QUANTIZER, not of reduction order in isolation. Re-run every arm
with --no-default-features before publishing a number. Relatedly the doctrine line
"Activations are f32" is stale: the default build quantizes activations to int8 and
back before every BitLinear.
